    "summary": "Resistance to data centers in Alabama is concentrated in counties that have already adopted moratoria: Baldwin (county and Foley), Walker (extended 180 days), Morgan (one-year, extended, though unenforceable under Dillon's Rule), and Shelby, where Chelsea, Harpersville, Westover and Helena all paused development. Organized opposition stands out in Jefferson (Bessemer groups, where two council members who backed the rezoning lost their seats), Talladega (685 petition signatures and a pending moratorium push), Lowndes (lawsuits against $2.7B in abatements for the Red Clay campus) and Escambia (commissioners unanimously opposed a 2,700-acre project). Counter-signals include Chilton rescinding its moratorium, Tuscaloosa approving projects with abatements, and Montgomery's leaders promoting data centers, while many counties have little or no local evidence."
  },
